 "conclusion": "At 1500 the atlas paints the untouched Cliopatria geometry (native bootstrap) immediately, so neither the build-time simplification nor the renderer is responsible for the stair-stepped outlines in the reported view. The reported outlines are what the source contains: Duchy of Bavaria has 55 vertices, median segment 19.0 km, 11/54 exactly axis-aligned; Swiss Confederation has 68 vertices, median segment 14.6 km, 20/65 exactly axis-aligned; Duchy of Lorraine has 102 vertices, median segment 14.1 km, 32/95 exactly axis-aligned. Simplification is a transient, not a steady state: on years without a native bootstrap pack the first overview paint can sit up to 4.41 km from source, and is then replaced by the untouched native geometry once data/native/index.json.gz and the per-geometry pieces load (verified in a real browser at 1503: 137/137 active records native after ~12 s). Portrayal (border curves, halo casing, line weight, fill opacity) only changes how those same coordinates look; it is reversible and documented, and 3.10 added a light casing under the historical line so the source outline stays readable over the modern relief instead of being muted by it.",
